WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.840
A new space telescope is on its way, the next Artemis mission is taking shape, and station

00:04.840 --> 00:06.960
crews are staying busy in orbit.

00:06.960 --> 00:09.720
Here's what's new this week in your NASA Minute.

00:09.720 --> 00:14.220
NASA's Nancy Grace-Roman Space Telescope is officially on its way.

00:14.220 --> 00:17.840
Roman launched Sunday morning from Kennedy Space Center aboard a SpaceX Falcon Heavy

00:17.840 --> 00:22.560
rocket, beginning a three-month, million-mile journey to its final orbit.

00:22.560 --> 00:26.920
With its large field of view and fast survey speeds, Roman will usher us into a new era

00:26.920 --> 00:31.520
of discovery and make the invisible visible, setting the foundation for humanity's search

00:31.520 --> 00:34.040
for life beyond our solar system.

00:34.040 --> 00:38.720
Following launch, President Trump called into NASA's post-launch press conference to congratulate

00:38.720 --> 00:43.080
the team and celebrate the mission as a major step forward for American leadership in space

00:43.080 --> 00:44.640
and science.

00:44.640 --> 00:48.120
NASA is making major progress towards Artemis 3.

00:48.120 --> 00:53.200
Teams at Kennedy Space Center are installing the RS-25 engines on the SLS core stage, stacking

00:53.200 --> 00:57.840
the rocket's boosters and powering on Orion to test connections between its crew and service

00:57.840 --> 00:58.840
modules.

00:58.840 --> 01:03.920
Meanwhile, at Johnson Space Center, the Artemis 3 crew is training in Orion simulators and

01:03.920 --> 01:08.000
practicing water landing scenarios at the Neutral Buoyancy Lab.

01:08.000 --> 01:12.280
Stay tuned as the hardware, spacecraft, and crew continue coming together for Artemis 3

01:12.280 --> 01:13.280
in mid-2027.

01:13.280 --> 01:18.160
And finally, it's been a busy stretch of spacewalks aboard the International Space

01:18.160 --> 01:19.160
Station.

01:19.440 --> 01:24.520
On Tuesday, NASA astronaut Jessica Meir and ESA astronaut Sophie Adano installed a new

01:24.520 --> 01:29.640
navigation reflector, swapped out a high-definition camera, and completed other maintenance tasks

01:29.640 --> 01:32.840
during the third spacewalk in three weeks.

01:32.840 --> 01:36.360
Summer may be winding down, but NASA's momentum is only picking up.

01:36.360 --> 01:38.320
We'll see you next time on the NASA Minute.

